Lab 15: Blind SQL Injection with Time Delays and
Information Retrieval
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab builds directly on lab 14. In lab 14, we only proved that we could make the database pause for a fixed
ten seconds, with no actual data extraction involved. This lab takes that same idea and turns it into a full
data-stealing technique, the same way conditional responses and conditional errors were eventually used to
extract real passwords in earlier labs. Instead of always sleeping for ten seconds no matter what, the sleep now
only happens when a specific condition we choose turns out to be true.

Goal: using only response timing as a signal, extract the administrator’s password character by character, then
log in as administrator.

==============================
WHERE THE INJECTION POINT IS

Same as lab 14, the vulnerable input is the TrackingId cookie, used inside a SQL query behind the scenes without
being checked or cleaned.

=============================
THE KEY IDEA: MAKING THE DELAY CONDITIONAL

In lab 14, the payload always called pg_sleep with ten, regardless of anything else, just to prove the
injection point could control timing at all. This lab wraps that same function inside a CASE WHEN structure,
exactly like the conditional error lab did with the divide by zero trick. Now, pg_sleep with ten only runs when
our chosen condition is true. If the condition is false, pg_sleep with zero runs instead, which causes no
meaningful delay at all, so the response comes back almost immediately.

This means a slow response becomes our “true” signal, and a fast response becomes our “false” signal, giving us
a reliable way to ask the database any true or false question we want, purely through timing.

=============================
STEP 1: CONFIRM A TRUE CONDITION CAUSES A DELAY

We first test with a condition we already know is true, one equals one, wrapped inside the CASE WHEN structure.
Sending this causes the application to take about ten seconds to respond, confirming the conditional sleep logic
works correctly.

=============================
STEP 2: CONFIRM A FALSE CONDITION CAUSES NO DELAY

Next, we test with a condition we know is false, one equals two. This time the application responds almost
immediately, confirming that only a true condition triggers the delay, and a false one does not.

=============================
STEP 3: CONFIRM THE ADMINISTRATOR USER EXISTS

Using the same structure, we now check if a row exists where the username equals administrator, this time
selecting from the real users table instead of nowhere in particular. A ten second delay confirms this user
exists.

=============================
STEP 4: FIND THE PASSWORD LENGTH

Still using the same structure, we repeatedly ask if the administrator’s password length is greater than one,
then two, then three, and so on, each time checking whether the response is slow or fast. The exact point where
the delay disappears tells us the real password length, confirmed in this lab as twenty characters.

=============================
STEP 5: FIND EACH CHARACTER, ONE POSITION AT A TIME

Once the length is known, we use SUBSTRING to test one character at a specific position against a guessed
letter or digit, combined with the administrator username check using AND. If the guess is correct, the delay
happens. If wrong, the response stays fast. We repeat this for every position, trying every lowercase letter and
digit until the correct one is found, then move to the next position.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and intercept a request containing the TrackingId cookie using Burp Suite.
Test a CASE WHEN structure with a true condition, confirming a ten second delay occurs.
Test the same structure with a false condition, confirming no delay occurs.
Confirm the administrator user exists using the same structure, selecting from the users table.
Test increasing password lengths until the delay disappears, revealing the password length.
Use Burp Intruder, limited to one request at a time for reliable timing, to test every character at every
position using SUBSTRING, watching the response time column for values around ten thousand milliseconds.
Once every position’s character is found, combine them into the full password, and log in manually as
administrator using that password.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, the time library for measuring elapsed time, the string library for easy
access to lowercase letters and digits, and a function called quote from the urllib.parse library, used for
manually encoding special characters.

It asks for the base address of the lab, and a session is created, then a request is sent to the home page to
obtain a genuine TrackingId cookie, saved for building every later payload on top of it.

A helper function called send_condition is defined. It builds the full payload using the original tracking ID, a
single quote, a semicolon, a SELECT CASE WHEN statement using the given condition, pg_sleep with ten in the true
branch, pg_sleep with zero in the false branch, FROM users, and two dashes at the end.

Since a semicolon has special meaning inside a cookie header, separating one cookie from another, this payload
is manually encoded using the quote function before being sent, converting every special character including
the semicolon into its safe, percent-encoded form, and the result is sent as a raw Cookie header instead of
using the normal cookies dictionary, which does not perform this kind of encoding automatically.

The function records the time just before sending the request, sends it, then records the time again right
after the response arrives, and returns whether the total elapsed time was nine seconds or more, allowing a
small buffer below the full ten seconds for natural network variation.

The script first confirms the administrator user exists using this helper function, stopping with an error if
this basic check fails.

It then searches for the password length, looping through increasing numbers and checking each time if the
length is greater than that number, stopping the moment a check comes back fast instead of slow, and saving that
number as the real password length.

It then begins the character extraction phase, trying every lowercase letter and digit at each position in the
password, combined with the administrator username check, and recording whichever character causes the ten
second delay as correct for that position, before moving on to the next position.

Once every position has been resolved, all the found characters are joined into the final complete password,
printed at the end, along with a reminder to log in manually using this password.

=============================
WHY THE COOKIE NEEDED MANUAL ENCODING THIS TIME

Unlike the earlier blind injection labs, this payload needed to include a semicolon as part of the actual SQL
syntax itself, since the attack works by stacking a brand new SELECT statement after the original query, rather
than just attaching an extra condition using AND. A semicolon inside a normal Cookie header is a reserved
character that separates multiple cookies from each other, so sending it as-is inside a cookie value would
either get cut off or misinterpreted by the server, rather than reaching the database as part of our intended
SQL text. Manually percent-encoding the entire payload before sending it as a raw header avoids this problem,
ensuring the semicolon survives intact all the way through to the vulnerable query.

=============================
WHY THIS TECHNIQUE CAN BE LESS RELIABLE THAN OTHER BLIND METHODS

Timing-based detection depends on measuring real-world elapsed time, which can be affected by things completely
unrelated to our injected condition, such as temporary network slowness, server load, or other minor delays.
This means there is a small chance of occasionally getting a false reading at some position, which can require
re-testing a position if the final extracted password turns out to be incorrect, something that did happen
during this lab’s extraction and required a second attempt before the correct password was found.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw remains the same root cause as every earlier blind injection lab, a cookie value reaching a
SQL query without being checked or cleaned. The specific technique here combines two earlier ideas together, the
CASE WHEN conditional structure from the error-based lab, and the pg_sleep timing function from the previous
lab, to create a fully controllable, purely time-based signal for extracting real data.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that cookie values can never change the structure or logic of a SQL query,
regardless of whether the attacker is trying to trigger a visible difference, an error, or a timing delay.

Other good practices:

Restrict or disable unnecessary database functions like pg_sleep for the account used by the web application,
since a normal application almost never has a legitimate reason to deliberately pause its own queries.
Monitor for unusually slow or repeated requests from the same source, since a time-based blind injection attack
typically requires sending a very large number of requests over a noticeably longer period compared to normal
application usage.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

A conditional sleep function turns timing itself into a reliable true or false signal, useful when no other
observable difference exists in the application’s response.
Special characters required by certain SQL syntax, such as a semicolon used to stack a new statement, may need
to be manually encoded when placed inside cookie headers, since cookies do not always get encoded automatically
the way URL parameters do.
Timing-based techniques can occasionally produce unreliable results due to natural variation in network and
server response times, so a wrong result during extraction does not necessarily mean the underlying logic is
flawed, it may simply need to be re-attempted.
This lab represents the most advanced form of blind SQL injection covered in this series, combining conditional
logic, string concatenation, and timing-based detection, all stacked together into a single working technique.
